INSERT INTO qa VALUES ('qa-2ab6b6c35f6c','cadw','Cadw / Welsh Government','welsh-government-and-cadw-email-correspondence-catalogue#t2','2026-02-06','*Archaeological Suppression:* In 1888, my family discovered a Roman soldier in full armor beneath the living room floorboards. This discovery provided "actual notice" of the site''s national significance a century before its demolition. We allege this notice was systematically suppressed by corporate and state actors to allow for commercial development. 2.

   *Fraudulent Misrepresentation in Planning:* During the 1987 planning process, the 13th-century fortified manor was described by developers as a "dilapidated utility building" or "ruined barn" to secure a demolition permit that would have otherwise been denied under heritage protection laws. 3.

   *Legal Identity Falsification:* In 1974, BP Pension Trust issued a "unilateral license" intended to bypass our possessory title. This correspondence was falsely addressed to "Mrs. Buckler" to bind my grandmother to her deceased husband’s name and legal status, despite her public rejection of their claim. She used her maiden name Williams and her husband Bucklers'' intent to accept license died with him. My nan also alledgedly didn''t expressly deny the offer and so accepted through her silence. But she was near enough voiceless from screaming for decades that she will not do deals with any of them. That''s a different kind of silence, if any. And to have responded to license to Mrs Buckler would have been identify fraud as she was Mrs Williams. And she did in fact reject BPs license offer and advanced, countless times, but publica
You''ll also see in landmark legal case BP Vs Buckler 1987 the judge says that she might have responded to BP, but to BP Pension Trust, but the claimant was BP Properties Ltd which were two separate companies. But in my dads appeal when he challenges that BP Pension Trust Ltd should have been the claimant the same judge dismissed his appeal citing both BP companies are effectively the same. This is clearly a case of heads BP won, Tails we lost. 5.

   *Missing Evidence:* Critical title deeds and','2026-02-25','Dear Sion,

Thank you for your email regarding Great House Farm, Llandough. However, I must inform you that much of the content in your correspondence falls outside the scope of Cadw’s work.

In relation to the claim of ''archaeological suppression'' involving the discovery of a Roman soldier in full armour in 1888, any such find would typically have been reported in the antiquarian press at either the national or local level. It is possible that records of this nature may be held by local museums. It is worth noting that the first Ancient Monuments Act was passed in 1882, focusing solely on bringing threatened ancient monuments into guardianship. The legal recognition of nationally important archaeology did not come about until the Ancient Monuments Consolidation and Amendment Act of 1913.

Upon reviewing the Cof Cymru database, I found that there are no designated historic assets relevant to this topic. However, Archwilio contains two records that document the involvement of the Glamorgan-Gwent Archaeological Trust (GGAT):

1. Great House Farm HER site entry - GGAT03586s
2. Land at Great House Farm HER event entry - GGATE003872, which includes GGAT''s 1990 archaeological assessment report commissioned by BP Properties Ltd.

Any advice or correspondence that Cadw may have provided should be preserved in the Royal Commission on the Ancient and Historical Monuments of Wales (RCAHMW) National Monuments Record of Wales (NMRW). I recommend you contact the NMRW to access any relevant documents. Additionally, you may want to reach out to Heneb and the Vale of Glamorgan Local Planning Authority (LPA) to explore what records they might hold concerning this matter.

Kind regards,

Jenna Arnold

Jenna Arnold
Arolygiaeth yr Amgylchedd Hanesyddol / Historic Environment Inspectorate
Llywodraeth Cymru / Welsh Government
Cadw, Rhydycar Business Park | Parc Business Rhydycar, Merthyr Tydfil | Merthyr Tudful CF48 1UZ

Ymunwch â Cadw<https://www.aelodaethcadw.gwasanaeth.llyw.cymru/>
Join Cadw<https://www.cadwmembership.service.gov.wales/>','redirected','thread','','https://greathousefarmwiki.wordpress.com/records-access-chronology/foi-reply-register/welsh-government-and-cadw-email-correspondence-catalogue/#t2','cq-1881-name,cq-armour,cq-1974');
